==========================================================================
 WHAT EACH PROBE PROVES
==========================================================================
 PROBE 1  H-2, R-131 · The defect, exactly. No system prompt was sent and
          none was injected, so the model answered from its own weights:
          "Vikram Deshpande did his PhD in Engineering at the University of
          Cambridge."  The CV of record carries TWO education records —
          Master of Computer Science, Monash University (2010) and Bachelor
          of Engineering, University of Melbourne (2007). Neither is a
          doctorate; Cambridge appears nowhere in the corpus. One sentence,
          two fabrications, HTTP 200.

 PROBE 2  H-2, R-73 · A client-supplied system prompt is accepted verbatim
          and obeyed. The bot introduced itself as "Captain Blackbeak
          Billy". Any visitor can strip the grounding by editing one JSON
          body, because the grounding is composed in the browser
          (lib/miniVicBrain.ts:189-206) and POSTed as messages[0].

 PROBE 3  R-131 · The model declined this one. Note WHY: not because a
          retrieval gate refused it, but because the model happened not to
          know a Vikram Deshpande at Google. Nothing in the server made that
          decision, so nothing guarantees it repeats. P-03 in the battery
          therefore asserts the mechanism, not the outcome.

 PROBE 4  R-131 · Same: the model answered as an assistant persona rather
          than being blocked by a validator. It also broke voice entirely
          ("I'm an AI designed to provide general information"), which is
          its own R-68 defect.

 PROBE 5  H-3, R-71 · content-type: application/json and content-length: 629.
          A Content-Length is the proof there is no stream: the whole
          upstream completion is awaited and returned as one blob
          (functions/index.js:159-176). Perceived first token == full round
          trip == 1.12 s measured on probe 1.

 PROBE 6  R-71 · /api/chat/stream returns HTTP 404. There is no streaming
          transport to select, so §4.1's probe has nothing to measure yet.

 PROBE 7  §3.1 · The AskRequest contract does not exist. The endpoint
          answers {"error":"messages_required"} — it accepts only the
          OpenAI-style {messages} envelope, which is precisely the shape
          that makes a system prompt representable in the request.

